# UI and UX

- Calm developer-tool look: neutral surfaces, one accent (`primary`), status
  colours only for status (`success`, `warning`, `danger`).
- Colours come from the tokens in `web/src/index.css` (`bg-card`,
  `text-muted-foreground`, `border`...). No raw hex or Tailwind palette colours
  in components. Both light and dark must read well; check both.
- Components follow shadcn/ui patterns: `cva` variants, `cn()` for classes,
  files in `web/src/components/ui/`.
- Every icon-only button has an accessible name (`sr-only` text or
  `aria-label`). Status icons have labels. Errors use `role="alert"`.
- Long jobs show progress steps and keep the output one click away. Never a
  blocking modal during a run.
- Destructive or risky actions ask inline before running, with the fix's
  `confirm` text.
- Layouts work from 375 px wide: stack on small screens, no horizontal scroll.
- Keyboard first: visible focus, Enter submits, actions reachable by Tab.

## One pattern on every page

- **A job shows where it was started**: under (or inside) the card whose
  button started it, scrolled into view, with its steps, Retry when it failed
  and the link to Undo once it settled (`JobBox` in
  `web/src/components/job-box.tsx`). A job started from a row's menu in a
  long list shows at the top of that list. The header's jobs indicator is the
  view of every job; a page never adds a second place for the same job.
- **A form with several fields saves through `SaveBar`**: it sticks to the
  bottom of the window while there are changes ("2 unsaved changes",
  Discard, and the one button that saves or applies, named for what it does:
  "Save and restart Apache", "Apply"). A single switch or button acts at once.
- **Every card that changes something says what it applies to** with
  `ScopeLabel` in its header: "all sites", "PHP 8.3", "this site".
- **An inherited value always shows its number and where it comes from**:
  "Inherit: 128M (php.ini)", "Inherit: 2M (PHP's built-in default)". Never a
  bare "default".
- **Global things sit above per-item things.** A page about many items (PHP
  versions, databases) starts with what applies to all of them, then the item
  you picked, under its own heading.
- **One word for one thing.** A site with a PHP version or settings of its
  own has its "own PHP"; the version every other site runs is "the default".
- **Confirm dialogs**: the title asks the question, the description says
  what happens and what Undo does, Cancel on the left, the action named for
  what it does on the right.
